# The ledger of `xml`: what Go's `encoding/xml` (Go 1.27) and Python's
# `xml.etree.ElementTree`, `xml.dom.minidom` and `xml.sax.saxutils` (3.15) let
# a program do, one block per capability, and what Rebel answers. Of minidom,
# only what is a capability stands in a block; the DOM's interfaces are in
# `dropped`. Rust's standard library has no XML, so its line is `-` in every
# block. The lists are this machine's, 2026-11-15: `go doc -all encoding/xml`,
# `ElementTree.__all__` with `dir` of its classes, and `dir` of `minidom`, its
# node classes and `saxutils`. Every expected value in the package's tests was
# observed from Python or Go on the same day, or decided by the standard where
# they disagree, as each block's note says.

capability xml/tree  a document held as a tree a program can match on
  heroes  xml.Document, xml.Element, xml.Node, xml.Misc, xml.Attribute
  go      Token, StartElement, EndElement, CharData, Comment, ProcInst, Attr
  rust    -
  python  ElementTree, ElementTree.getroot, Element, Element.tag, Element.text, Element.tail, Element.attrib, minidom.Document, minidom.Element, minidom.Text, minidom.Comment, minidom.ProcessingInstruction, minidom.Attr, minidom.CharacterData, Document.documentElement, Node.childNodes, Element.tagName, ProcessingInstruction.target, ProcessingInstruction.data, CharacterData.data, Attr.name, Attr.value
  note    a document holds its declaration, the comments and instructions before and after its one root, and the root; an element holds its children in order, text among them, where ElementTree splits text into `text` and `tail`. A `match` on a `Node` answers all four cases. The variants and records are the face's, decided 2026-11-16

capability xml/name  a name with its prefix, its local name and the namespace it resolves to
  heroes  xml.Name, xml.format_name, xml.XMLNS_SPACE, xml.XML_SPACE
  go      Name, Name.Space, Name.Local
  rust    -
  python  QName, Node.prefix, Node.localName, Node.namespaceURI, minidom.EMPTY_NAMESPACE, minidom.EMPTY_PREFIX, minidom.XMLNS_NAMESPACE
  note    a name keeps its prefix, its local name and its namespace, as minidom's `prefix`, `localName` and `namespaceURI` and Go's `Name` (whose `Space` is the namespace); a declaration's own name is in `XMLNS_SPACE`, as minidom places it, where Go puts `xmlns` in `Space`. ElementTree's `{uri}local` drops the prefix

capability xml/parse  a text read into a document, by XML 1.0 and Namespaces in XML 1.0
  heroes  xml.parse, xml.parse_with
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  fromstring, XML, fromstringlist, XMLParser, XMLParser.feed, XMLParser.close, XMLParser.flush, XMLParser.version, minidom.parseString
  note    XML 1.0 (fifth edition) and Namespaces in XML 1.0, well-formedness checked whole: names, a start tag matched by its end tag, one root, an attribute once, `]]>` outside CDATA, `--` in a comment, an instruction's target `xml` in any case, a character XML cannot hold, a prefix not declared. Go's token reader passes two roots, an undeclared prefix and an attribute twice; ElementTree refuses all of them, and so does this. A line end is read as XML 1.0 section 2.11 says, an attribute's value normalised as section 3.3.3 says, as ElementTree does; Go keeps a value's tabs and line ends. A CDATA section is read as text, and text side by side as one text

capability xml/parse-bytes  bytes read into a document, which may not be UTF-8
  heroes  xml.decode
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  fromstring (of bytes), minidom.parseString (of bytes)
  note    bytes that are not UTF-8 fail `not_text`, the message giving the line and column of the first byte that is not, columns counted from 1 where ElementTree counts from 0

capability xml/parse-where-wrong  where a text stops being XML, for a program to act on
  heroes  xml.parse
  go      SyntaxError, SyntaxError.Msg, SyntaxError.Line, SyntaxError.Error, Decoder.InputPos
  rust    -
  python  ParseError, ParseError.code, ParseError.position
  note    a failure is `invalid` and its message opens `line L, column C:`, both counted from 1 and the column in characters, then says what was expected and what was found; ElementTree counts the column from 0, Go gives the line alone

capability xml/parse-limits  a limit on depth and on size the caller sets
  heroes  xml.ParseOptions, xml.default_options, xml.DEFAULT_MAX_DEPTH, xml.DEFAULT_MAX_BYTES
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    past either limit `limit`; neither reference has one. The depth is held because `==` follows a tree's nesting (512 levels, as `json`); the size because it bounds what a text from outside may cost, no entity being expanded (64 MiB by default). The size is the whole bound, since no entity is expanded

capability xml/well-formed  whether a text is XML
  heroes  xml.is_well_formed
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    true exactly where `parse` succeeds

capability xml/references  the five predefined entities and character references read
  heroes  xml.parse, xml.unescape
  go      Decoder.Entity (its five standard entries)
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    `&lt;`, `&gt;`, `&amp;`, `&quot;`, `&apos;` and every character reference to a character XML holds; any other entity is `invalid`, as both refuse it

capability xml/entities-declared  an entity a DTD or the caller declares, expanded
  heroes  refused: an entity expanded is how a document makes a reader grow without end (the billion laughs), so none is; decided 2026-11-16
  go      Decoder.Entity (entries of the caller's)
  rust    -
  python  XMLParser.entity, minidom.Entity, minidom.Notation
  note    a DTD's internal subset that declares an entity is `invalid` with a message, where ElementTree expands it; an entity the caller would name, Go's `Decoder.Entity`, is refused for the same reason

capability xml/doctype  the document type declaration, its name and identifiers kept
  heroes  refused: no DTD is processed (decided 2026-11-16), so nothing in a document type declaration changes the document read and the tree keeps nothing of it, as ElementTree keeps nothing
  go      Directive, Directive.Copy
  rust    -
  python  minidom.DocumentType, Document.doctype, DocumentType.name, DocumentType.publicId, DocumentType.systemId, DocumentType.internalSubset
  note    `parse` reads and checks it: its name, its external identifier, and an internal subset of element and notation declarations, comments and instructions. An attribute list or a parameter entity reference is `invalid` as well as an entity, since its defaults and types would change the attributes read and are not applied; ElementTree applies them

capability xml/external  a resource a document names, fetched
  heroes  refused: nothing a document names is ever fetched (decided 2026-11-16)
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  saxutils.prepare_input_source
  note    -

capability xml/encodings  a document in another encoding than UTF-8, read or written
  heroes  refused: UTF-8 alone is read and written (decided 2026-11-16)
  go      Decoder.CharsetReader
  rust    -
  python  XMLParser(encoding=), tostring(encoding=), ElementTree.write(encoding=), Node.toxml(encoding=), Node.toprettyxml(encoding=), Document.encoding, Document.actualEncoding
  note    a declaration naming another encoding fails `unsupported`, where ElementTree reads Latin-1 and Go asks its `CharsetReader`; `format` fails `invalid` on a declaration naming another

capability xml/declaration  the XML declaration read and written: its version, encoding and standalone
  heroes  xml.Declaration, xml.document
  go      Header, ProcInst (of target xml)
  rust    -
  python  tostring(xml_declaration=), ElementTree.write(xml_declaration=), Document.version, Document.standalone, Node.toxml(standalone=), Node.toprettyxml(standalone=)
  note    kept as written, version, encoding and standalone. A version is `1.` and digits as XML 1.0 writes it, `1.1` read as 1.0 as its section 2.8 says; `2.0` is `invalid`, as Go refuses it (`unsupported version`) and ElementTree reads it. `document` opens with Go's `Header` without its line break

capability xml/namespaces  each name resolved to its namespace by the declarations in scope
  heroes  xml.parse, xml.Name
  go      Name.Space (as Decoder.Token resolves it)
  rust    -
  python  the `{uri}local` tags of ElementTree, Node.namespaceURI
  note    each declaration stands for its element and everything inside; `xml` is bound without being declared; `xmlns:p=""`, `xmlns` declared, `xml` bound elsewhere and two attributes of one expanded name are `invalid` as Namespaces in XML 1.0 and ElementTree refuse them

capability xml/default-space  a namespace given to every unprefixed name, as if the document declared it
  heroes  refused: a document's namespaces are those it declares, so a reading that adds one would answer names the text does not hold
  go      Decoder.DefaultSpace
  rust    -
  python  -
  note    -

capability xml/namespace-registry  a prefix chosen once, for every later write, for a namespace
  heroes  refused: a prefix chosen for every later write is a mutable global; a prefix is declared on an element with `with_namespace`
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  register_namespace
  note    -

capability xml/children  an element's child elements: by name, the first, every one
  heroes  xml.child, xml.child_in, xml.children_named, xml.children_in, xml.child_elements
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  Element.find, Element.findall, Element.iterfind (of one name), minidom Node.firstChild, Node.lastChild, Node.hasChildNodes
  note    by the name as written, or by namespace and local name whatever the prefix, as ElementTree's `{uri}local`; absent is `not_found` naming the children there are, where ElementTree answers None

capability xml/descendants  every element inside another, by name or every one, in document order
  heroes  xml.elements_named, xml.descendants_in, xml.descendants
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  Element.iter, ElementTree.iter, Element.getElementsByTagName, Element.getElementsByTagNameNS, Document.getElementsByTagName, Document.getElementsByTagNameNS
  note    document order; the element itself is left out, as minidom's getElementsByTagName leaves it and ElementTree's iter does not. On the package's own stack

capability xml/by-id  an element found by its identifier
  heroes  xml.descendants, xml.attribute
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  XMLID, Document.getElementById
  note    an identifier is whatever attribute the program names, since no DTD types one: a program walks `descendants` and asks `attribute`

capability xml/path  an element reached by a path of names
  heroes  xml.element_at, xml.elements_at
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  ElementTree.find, ElementTree.findall, ElementTree.iterfind, ElementTree.findtext, Element.findtext, Element.find, Element.findall and Element.iterfind (of a path)
  note    names as written separated by `/`; `element_at` answers the first in document order and `elements_at` all of them, as ElementTree's find and findall. The empty path reaches the element itself, where ElementTree's find answers None

capability xml/xpath  XPath: wildcards, parents, predicates, positions
  heroes  refused: XPath is a language of its own, of axes, predicates and functions; a path of names reaches what walking a tree needs, the rest being written in the program, and `*`, `.`, `..`, `//`, `@` and `[` are `invalid` with a message saying so
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  Element.find, Element.findall and Element.iterfind given `*`, `.`, `..`, `//` or a `[...]` predicate
  note    -

capability xml/attributes  an attribute by name, with or without a namespace, and whether it is there
  heroes  xml.attribute, xml.attribute_in, xml.has_attribute, xml.Element
  go      StartElement.Attr
  rust    -
  python  Element.get, Element.keys, Element.items, minidom Element.getAttribute, Element.getAttributeNS, Element.hasAttribute, Element.hasAttributeNS, Element.hasAttributes, Element.getAttributeNode, Element.getAttributeNodeNS, Element.attributes
  note    absent is `not_found` naming the attributes there are, where ElementTree answers None and minidom the empty text; the attributes are the element's field, in their order

capability xml/text  the text an element holds
  heroes  xml.text_of
  go      CharData
  rust    -
  python  Element.itertext, tostring(method="text"), Text.wholeText
  note    every text inside, at any depth, joined, as ElementTree's itertext and minidom's textContent; a program wanting only an element's own text matches its children

capability xml/text-merged  text side by side held as one text
  heroes  xml.parse
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  Node.normalize
  note    `parse` never answers two texts side by side; a built tree's are written as one text and read back as one

capability xml/build  a document built by a program: elements, attributes, text, comments, instructions
  heroes  xml.element, xml.with_attribute, xml.without_attribute, xml.with_text, xml.with_child, xml.document
  go      StartElement{...}, Name{...}, Attr{...}, CharData(...), Comment(...), ProcInst{...}
  rust    -
  python  Element(), SubElement, Element.makeelement, Element.set, Element.append, Element.extend, Element.insert, Element.remove, Element.clear, Comment, PI, ProcessingInstruction, TreeBuilder, TreeBuilder.start, TreeBuilder.end, TreeBuilder.data, TreeBuilder.comment, TreeBuilder.pi, TreeBuilder.close, Document.createElement, Document.createTextNode, Document.createComment, Document.createProcessingInstruction, Document.createAttribute, Document.createDocumentFragment, Element.setAttribute, Element.setAttributeNode, Element.removeAttribute, Element.removeAttributeNode, Node.appendChild, Node.insertBefore, Node.removeChild, Node.replaceChild, Document.renameNode
  note    the records and the cases are constructors too; a list of children is edited with the built-ins and given to `children` once, since each `with_` copies the element

capability xml/build-namespaces  a namespaced name and a namespace declaration built by a program
  heroes  xml.element_in, xml.with_attribute_in, xml.with_namespace
  go      Name{Space: ...} (as Encoder.EncodeToken declares it)
  rust    -
  python  Element("{uri}local"), Document.createElementNS, Document.createAttributeNS, Element.setAttributeNS, Element.setAttributeNodeNS, Element.removeAttributeNS, Element.removeAttributeNodeNS
  note    a name carries its namespace and `format` holds it to the declarations in scope, failing `invalid` with the declaration to add, where Go and ElementTree invent prefixes (`ns0`)

capability xml/format  a document or an element written as text on one line
  heroes  xml.format, xml.format_with, xml.format_element
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  tostring, tostringlist, ElementTree.write, dump, Node.toxml, Node.writexml
  note    the same tree always the same bytes, and those bytes read back as the same tree: attributes in their order between `"`, an empty element `<a/>` as minidom writes it. Where reading would not give the tree back, `invalid`: a comment holding `--` (ElementTree writes it), a character XML cannot hold (ElementTree writes it, Go writes U+FFFD), a carriage return in a comment or an instruction, a name or a prefix that does not hold. Writing takes time linear in the tree since 2026-11-18: each element's place (`/a/a/.../a`, for a message) was built for every start tag, comment and instruction, so a tree a hundred thousand deep wrote 20 GB of paths and took 108 s on this Mac; it is built now only where a message names it. Measured on this Mac, the test "with_child: a tree a hundred thousand deep" plain 124 s before and 0.46 s after (110 MB), and under `--sanitize` stopped past 470 s and 1.8 GB before (ASan keeping the freed paths: 1.3 GB at 12,500 deep, 38 MB with its quarantine set to 0) and 1.9 s and 366 MB after, of which 134 MB stay with no quarantine

capability xml/format-indented  a document written over several lines, indented
  heroes  xml.format_indented, xml.FormatOptions
  go      Encoder.Indent
  rust    -
  python  indent, Node.toprettyxml
  note    an element whose children are elements, comments and instructions with white space between gets one child to a line, and one holding other text is written on one line as it is, as ElementTree's indent lays them out; no line break ends the text

capability xml/format-empty  an element with no content written `<a/>` or `<a></a>`
  heroes  xml.FormatOptions
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  tostring(short_empty_elements=), ElementTree.write(short_empty_elements=)
  note    `self_closing`, true in `compact`; `<a/>` as minidom writes it, where ElementTree writes `<a />` and Go `<a></a>`

capability xml/escape  a text escaped to stand as content or as an attribute's value, and read back
  heroes  xml.escape_text, xml.quote_attribute, xml.unescape
  go      EscapeText, Escape
  rust    -
  python  saxutils.escape, saxutils.quoteattr, saxutils.unescape
  note    text escapes `&`, `<`, `>` and a carriage return, which saxutils' escape leaves raw and reading would change; a value adds `"`, tab and line feed, as quoteattr, always between double quotes where quoteattr may choose single ones. A character XML cannot hold fails `invalid`. `unescape` reads the five entities and character references, where saxutils reads three, and refuses any other

capability xml/canonical  the one text Canonical XML gives a document
  heroes  pending
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  canonicalize, C14NWriterTarget, ElementTree.write_c14n, ElementTree.write(method="c14n")
  note    Canonical XML 2.0, which Python's canonicalize writes, is not written yet

capability xml/cdata-written  text written as a CDATA section
  heroes  refused: a CDATA section is read as text (decided 2026-11-16), so the tree holds none to write; text written escaped reads back as the same characters
  go      -
  rust    -
  python  Document.createCDATASection, minidom.CDATASection
  note    -

